{#- Deterministic (no-LLM) file page.

    Rendered straight from the assembled FilePageContext: no model call, every
    statement comes from the parse, the import graph, git history or the
    knowledge graph. It is read on the file view and embedded for search, so it
    serves both a reader and the index.

    For the reader it opens with prose (the docstring, or sentences built from
    what the file defines and who imports it) and follows with a compact
    reference. For the index every identifier the context carried stays in the
    text: symbols, neighbour paths and the layer are spelled in full. Sections
    written only for retrieval stay on the page and the reader personas in
    ``docs/reader-persona.ts`` hide them client-side.

    Every section below the Overview is conditional. A heading with nothing
    under it costs a reader a stop and puts the same stock sentence on
    thousands of pages. The Overview always says something, and
    ``_extract_summary`` reads its first paragraph back as the summary shown in
    search results, the wiki list and ``get_context``.
-#}
{%- set api_syms = ctx.symbols | api_symbols -%}
{%- set other_syms = ctx.symbols | internal_symbols -%}
{%- set git = ctx.git_metadata or {} -%}
{%- set facts = ctx | file_facts -%}
{#- "`a`, `b` and `c`", or "`a`, `b`, `c` and 4 more". -#}
{%- macro name_list(names, more=0) -%}
{%- set items = names + ([labels.and_count_more.format(count=more)] if more else []) -%}
{%- if items | length > 1 -%}{{ items[:-1] | join(", ") }} {{ labels.and_word }} {{ items[-1] }}{%- else -%}{{ items | join("") }}{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
{#- The opening, which is also the page summary the wiki list, search results
    and ``get_context`` show. The author's docstring leads when there is one;
    otherwise the first sentence names the file and says what it defines and
    who imports it, and a second paragraph says where it sits. Every sentence
    is conditional on the fact behind it. Sentences carry a ``{subject}`` slot:
    the page's first sentence names the file, the rest say "it". -#}
{%- set about = [] -%}
{%- set where = [] -%}
{%- if facts.defines -%}
{%- set _ = about.append(labels.file_defines.format(subject="{subject}", names=name_list(facts.defines, facts.defines_more))) -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- set imp = facts.importers -%}
{%- if imp.listed -%}
{%- set _ = about.append(labels.file_imported_by.format(subject="{subject}", names=name_list(imp.listed))) -%}
{%- elif imp.count -%}
{%- set _ = about.append(labels.file_imported_by_count.format(subject="{subject}", count=imp.count, tests=labels.of_them_tests.format(count=imp.tests) if imp.tests else "")) -%}
{%- if imp.all_in_one -%}
{%- set _ = about.append(labels.all_in_directory.format(directory=imp.directory)) -%}
{%- elif imp.directory -%}
{%- set _ = about.append((labels.largest_share_code if imp.tests else labels.largest_share).format(count=imp.share, directory=imp.directory)) -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- set dep = facts.imports -%}
{%- if dep.listed -%}
{%- set _ = where.append(labels.file_imports.format(subject="{subject}", names=name_list(dep.listed))) -%}
{%- elif dep.count -%}
{%- set _ = where.append(labels.file_imports_count.format(subject="{subject}", count=dep.count)) -%}
{%- if dep.all_in_one -%}
{%- set _ = where.append(labels.all_in_directory.format(directory=dep.directory)) -%}
{%- elif dep.directory -%}
{%- set _ = where.append(labels.largest_share.format(count=dep.share, directory=dep.directory)) -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- if ctx.kg_layer_name and ctx.kg_layer_role == "entry_point" -%}
{%- set _ = where.append(labels.file_layer_entry.format(subject="{subject}", layer=ctx.kg_layer_name)) -%}
{%- elif ctx.kg_layer_name and ctx.kg_layer_role == "edge_connector" -%}
{%- set _ = where.append(labels.file_layer_boundary.format(subject="{subject}", layer=ctx.kg_layer_name)) -%}
{%- elif ctx.kg_layer_name -%}
{%- set _ = where.append(labels.file_layer.format(subject="{subject}", layer=ctx.kg_layer_name)) -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- if ctx.is_entry_point and not (ctx.kg_layer_name and ctx.kg_layer_role == "entry_point") -%}
{%- set _ = where.append(labels.file_entry_point.format(subject="{subject}")) -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- if not (facts.purpose or about or where) -%}
{%- set _ = about.append(labels.file_fallback.format(subject="{subject}", article=labels.article_an if ctx.language and ctx.language[0] in "aeiou" else labels.article_a, language=ctx.language)) -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- set sentences = (about + where) | with_subject(labels.it_subject if facts.purpose else facts.name, labels.it_subject) -%}
{%- set paragraphs = [facts.purpose, sentences] if facts.purpose else [sentences[:about | length], sentences[about | length:]] -%}
# {{ ctx.file_path }}

## {{ labels.overview }}
{%- for paragraph in paragraphs if paragraph %}

{{ paragraph if paragraph is string else paragraph | join(" ") }}
{%- endfor %}
{%- if ctx.decision_records %}

## {{ labels.decisions_heading }}
{% for dr in ctx.decision_records[:3] %}
- **{{ dr.title | oneline(80) }}**: {{ dr.decision | oneline }}{% if dr.rationale %} *({{ dr.rationale | oneline(120) }})*{% endif %}
{%- endfor %}
{%- endif %}
{#- The declared surface as a list a reader scans: each symbol's declaration
    and the first sentence of its docstring, methods under their class.

    Names that are public to the parser without being anything a caller
    reaches for (``log``, ``__all__``) are declared under the list, not
    dropped, with their signatures: ``content`` is also the index entry, and
    ``log`` alone would take ``structlog`` and ``get_logger`` out of it.
-#}
{%- if api_syms or other_syms %}

## {{ labels.public_api }}
{%- if api_syms %}
{% for entry in api_syms | symbol_entries %}
- {{ entry.declared }}{% if entry.doc %}: {{ entry.doc }}{% endif %}
{%- for member in entry.members %}
  - {{ member.declared }}{% if member.doc %}: {{ member.doc }}{% endif %}
{%- endfor %}
{%- endfor %}
{%- endif %}
{%- if other_syms %}

{{ labels.also_defined.format(names=other_syms | declarations | map("code_span") | join(", ")) }}
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
{#- Import neighbourhoods, one line per directory. The opening already says
    how many and where most of them are; this is the lookup. Each path is
    printed whole: it is what the reader's link resolves and what a question
    matches in the index. -#}
{%- for heading, paths in [(labels.used_by, ctx.dependents), (labels.depends_on, ctx.dependencies)] if paths %}

## {{ heading }}
{% for group in paths | group_paths %}
- {{ group.paths | map("code_span") | join(", ") }}
{%- endfor %}
{%- if paths | length > 25 %}

_{{ labels.and_more.format(count=paths | length - 25) }}_
{%- endif %}
{%- endfor %}
{#- What the history knows and the code cannot say, as one short paragraph.

    Every clause is conditional on the number behind it, so a repository with
    no git history renders no heading rather than a row of zeroes. Dates are
    absolute, never "three days ago": the rendered bytes are this page's reuse
    key, and a relative date would restate every page in the wiki daily.
-#}
{%- set history = [] -%}
{%- set commits = git.get("commit_count_total") or 0 -%}
{%- if commits -%}
{%- set _ = history.append(labels.history_commits.format(total=commits, commit_word=labels.commit_plural if commits != 1 else labels.commit_singular, recent=git.get("commit_count_90d") or 0)) -%}
{%- if git.get("last_commit_at") -%}
{%- set _ = history.append(labels.history_last_commit.format(date=git.get("last_commit_at") | datestamp)) -%}
{%- endif -%}
{#- The column is written on either scale depending on the producer, the
    same normalisation ``health/biomarkers/developer_congestion.py`` applies.
    Unnormalised, a 0-100 source renders "at 8500% of commits". -#}
{#- With blame the owner is the top author of current lines, so the line share
    leads and the commit share is named as such; without blame both the owner
    and the share come from commits. -#}
{%- set owner_pct = git.get("primary_owner_commit_pct") -%}
{%- set line_pct = git.get("primary_owner_line_pct") -%}
{%- if git.get("primary_owner_name") and line_pct and owner_pct -%}
{%- set _ = history.append(labels.history_owner_lines.format(owner=git.get("primary_owner_name"), line=(line_pct if line_pct > 1.0 else line_pct * 100) | round | int, commit=(owner_pct if owner_pct > 1.0 else owner_pct * 100) | round | int)) -%}
{%- elif git.get("primary_owner_name") and line_pct -%}
{%- set _ = history.append(labels.history_owner_lines_only.format(owner=git.get("primary_owner_name"), line=(line_pct if line_pct > 1.0 else line_pct * 100) | round | int)) -%}
{%- elif git.get("primary_owner_name") and owner_pct -%}
{%- set _ = history.append(labels.history_owner.format(owner=git.get("primary_owner_name"), pct=(owner_pct if owner_pct > 1.0 else owner_pct * 100) | round | int)) -%}
{%- endif -%}
{#- Suppressed when it exceeds the commit total: the two are counted over
    different windows, and a file whose fix count outruns its own log is one
    that has been moved, so the fixes belong to a path this page no longer
    describes. -#}
{%- if git.get("prior_defect_count") and git.get("prior_defect_count") <= commits -%}
{%- set _ = history.append(labels.history_fixes.format(count=git.get("prior_defect_count"))) -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- if git.get("is_hotspot") -%}
{%- set _ = history.append(labels.history_hotspot) -%}
{%- elif git.get("is_stable") -%}
{%- set _ = history.append(labels.history_stable) -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- if history %}

## {{ labels.history }}

{{ history | join(" ") }}
{%- endif %}
{%- if ctx.co_change_pages %}

## {{ labels.changes_with }}

{{ labels.changes_with_intro }}
{% for cc in ctx.co_change_pages %}
- `{{ cc.path }}`: {{ labels.changes_with_entry.format(count=cc.commits, commit_word=labels.commit_plural if cc.commits != 1 else labels.commit_singular) }}{% if cc.last %} ({{ labels.last_together.format(date=cc.last | datestamp) }}){% endif %}
{%- endfor %}
{%- endif %}
{#- Question-shaped text, built from structure rather than prose.

    Every search query is a question and this page is a declarative reference,
    so the two share almost no vocabulary. The identifiers are the whole point:
    thousands of file pages asking the same question differ only in the file
    and symbol they name, and without those they would be one sentence
    repeated across the corpus, matching everything and distinguishing nothing.

    A question is emitted only where the page can answer it. A question whose
    answer is not below it puts the words of a promise into the index and then
    breaks it, which is the failure this block exists to avoid. Low on the
    page so it cannot become the summary ``_extract_summary`` reads back.

    Built as a list and capped, because a fully-connected file qualifies for
    more of them than are worth carrying: past three the block stops being
    question-shaped text and starts being padding on every page in the corpus.
-#}
{%- set questions = [] -%}
{%- if api_syms -%}
{%- set _ = questions.append(labels.question_exports.format(path=ctx.file_path)) -%}
{%- set _ = questions.append(labels.question_where_defined.format(symbol=api_syms[0].name)) -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- if ctx.dependents -%}
{%- set _ = questions.append(labels.question_what_imports.format(path=ctx.file_path)) -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- if ctx.dependencies -%}
{%- set _ = questions.append(labels.question_depends_on.format(path=ctx.file_path)) -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- if questions %}

## {{ labels.questions_heading }}
{% for q in questions[:3] %}
- {{ q }}
{%- endfor %}
{%- endif %}
{#- The file's own vocabulary (``ctx.file_vocabulary``) is not rendered.
    ``_render_file_page`` stores it in page metadata and ``embed_item`` adds
    it to the embedded text: it is a bag of words, which serves retrieval and
    reads to a person as noise.
#}

{% include "_footer.j2" %}
